  74: "# 74. Character Stream Class\n## 1. Overview of Java I/O Streams & Networking\nJava I/O is built around **Streams**—an abstraction that produces or consumes sequences of data bytes:\n- **Byte Streams** (`InputStream` / `OutputStream`): Process raw 8-bit binary data (images, PDFs, audio).\n- **Character Streams** (`Reader` / `Writer`): Process 16-bit Unicode characters (text files, strings).\n\n```\n                            JAVA I/O STREAMS\n                                   │\n              ┌────────────────────┴────────────────────┐\n              ▼                                         ▼\n         Byte Streams                           Character Streams\n    (InputStream / OutputStream)                (Reader / Writer)\n              │                                         │\n    FileInputStream, etc.                     FileReader, BufferedReader\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. File Reading & Writing with Try-With-Resources\n```java\nimport java.io.*;\n\npublic class FileIODemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        File file = new File(\"notes.txt\");\n\n        // Write to file:\n        try (BufferedWriter writer = new BufferedWriter(new FileWriter(file))) {\n            writer.write(\"Mastering Core Java I/O Streams!\");\n            writer.newLine();\n        } catch (IOException e) {\n            e.printStackTrace();\n        }\n\n        // Read from file:\n        try (BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(file))) {\n            String line;\n            while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {\n                System.out.println(\"Read: \" + line);\n            }\n        } catch (IOException e) {\n            e.printStackTrace();\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Java Socket Networking (TCP/IP Client-Server)\n- **`ServerSocket`**: Listens on a designated port for incoming client connection requests (`server.accept()`).\n- **`Socket`**: Represents the endpoint of a two-way TCP connection between client and server.\n\n---\n\n## 💡 Key Takeaway\nAlways wrap low-level file streams inside buffered wrappers (`BufferedReader` / `BufferedWriter`) to reduce disk I/O interrupts. Always use try-with-resources to guarantee stream closure.",

