  106: "# 106. Abstract Design Pattern\n## 1. Overview of Java Collections Framework\nThe **Java Collections Framework (JCF)** provides a unified architecture for storing, manipulating, and accessing groups of objects in memory.\n\n```\n                    JAVA COLLECTIONS FRAMEWORK HIERARCHY\n                                 Iterable\n                                     │\n                                 Collection\n                                     │\n           ┌─────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────┐\n           ▼                         ▼                         ▼\n         List                       Set                      Queue\n  (Ordered, Duplicates)     (Unique Elements)          (FIFO Ordering)\n           │                         │                         │\n     ┌─────┴─────┐             ┌─────┴─────┐                   │\n     ▼           ▼             ▼           ▼                   ▼\n ArrayList  LinkedList      HashSet     TreeSet           PriorityQueue\n                                     (Sorted / Red-Black)\n```\n> Note: **`Map`** (`HashMap`, `TreeMap`) is part of the Collections Framework but does not extend `Collection` because it deals with Key-Value pairs rather than standalone elements.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Master Comparison of Core Collections\n\n| Collection | Internal Structure | Ordered? | Duplicates? | Search / Access Time |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **`ArrayList`** | Resizable Dynamic Array | **Yes** (Index) | **Yes** | **O(1)** by index |\n| **`LinkedList`**| Doubly Linked List | **Yes** (Insertion)| **Yes** | **O(n)** search, O(1) ends |\n| **`HashSet`** | Hash Table (`HashMap`) | No | **No** | **O(1)** average |\n| **`TreeSet`** | Self-Balancing Red-Black Tree| **Yes** (Sorted)| **No** | **O(log n)** |\n| **`HashMap`** | Array of Hash Buckets | No | Keys: No, Values: Yes | **O(1)** average |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Fast Idiomatic Usage\n```java\nimport java.util.*;\n\n// 1. List with Generics\nList<String> names = new ArrayList<>();\nnames.add(\"Java\");\nnames.add(\"Spring\");\n\n// 2. HashMap Key-Value Storage\nMap<String, Integer> scores = new HashMap<>();\nscores.put(\"Alice\", 95);\nscores.put(\"Bob\", 88);\n\n// Iterating over Map entries:\nfor (Map.Entry<String, Integer> entry : scores.entrySet()) {\n    System.out.println(entry.getKey() + \": \" + entry.getValue());\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 💡 Key Takeaway\nDefault to **`ArrayList`** for sequences and **`HashMap`** for associative key-value lookups. When sorted unique data is required, use **`TreeSet`** / **`TreeMap`**.",

